Why chuspace?
In early 2015, when the medium was getting popular, the idea for chuspace was born. The initial idea was to make a blogging platform for developers, that provides developer like experience, unlike medium which is mostly UI and the writing experience is terrible when it comes to adding code. The only way in medium to get workable code was through gists and it still is.

Later, it felt that the problem is something completely different i.e. quality of the programming content. Now, most developers write on programming at some point in their career either casually or seriously and earlier most of them choose to do it on their own website however since medium came more and more developers are writing blog posts on medium but the quality of content hasn't improved yet because everyone is writing their own minds without any collaboration. For example: you get about 50 articles from beginners finding graphql for the first time, 5 articles for super specific use-cases that don't apply, and then you end up looking into the source to figure it out yourself.

On the other side, if we look into open source libraries we see that they are maintained continuously over time both by the authors and community and really good quality.

Problems
Editor experience - sharing code
Collaboration and maintainability
Fragmented community
Quality of content
Learning as whole
